All modal verbs (shall, should, will, would, can, could, may, might, must, had better) must be followed 
by the BASE FORM of the main verb — never "to + verb" (infinitive), and never an adjective.

The Structure

Modal verb + V1 (base form) — nothing else is allowed directly after a modal.

1. Modal + Infinitive (❌ wrong — "to" should NOT appear)

❌ You should to go to school on time.
✅ You should go to school on time.

❌ He can to swim very well.
✅ He can swim very well.

❌ They will to come tomorrow.
✅ They will come tomorrow.

Why: Modals already carry the grammatical "weight" that infinitives (to + verb) usually carry elsewhere. 
Adding "to" after a modal is redundant and wrong — modals go straight to the base verb, no "to" in between.

2. Modal + Adjective (❌ wrong — needs a verb, not a describing word)

❌ You should careful while driving.
✅ You should be careful while driving.
(Notice: you need the verb "be" inserted — "careful" alone is an adjective, not a verb, so it can't follow a modal directly.)

❌ He must honest in his dealings.
✅ He must be honest in his dealings.

Why: A modal must be followed by an actual verb (action or "be"). 
If what follows is naturally an adjective, you need to insert "be" as the base verb before it.
